SSSTRAVELTIME
+++++++++++++

SUMMARY
-------
Computes traveltime of selected phases for pre-defined velocity models
Description and examples assume the calls are made from within SSS.
To see the help file for the non-SSS version, go to TRAVELTIME_.

SYNTAX
------
::

   TRAVELTIME {MODEL string} {PICKS number} {PHASE phase list}  {VERBOSE | QUIET} {M |KM}

INPUT
-----

   :MODEL: iasp91, ak135

   :PICKS: There are a total of 10 time picks in the SAC header: t0 to
     t9.  If the number is n (0<=n<=9), the first phase will be at Tn.
     If  PICKS is not included among the command-line options, VERBOSE
     will be turned on and the phase arrival times will be displayed
     but will not be put in the header. The default is not to have
     PICKS on, so no arrival times will be put in the header.

   :PHASE: List of phases for which times are picked and displayed. If
     PICKS n is among the options, the phase arrivala times and their
     labels will be added to the header starting at Tn.  If n is 0, one
     can have up to 10 phases, if n is 8, one can have no more than 2.
     Phase names are case sensitive.  Using the CLEAR keyword with the
     phase list, clears the list of phases from previous commands.

   :VERBOSE | QUIET:  If VERBOSE is among the options in the TRAVELTIME
     command line, phase arrival times are displayed relative to both
     the origin time (O) and to the first-point time (B).  If QUIET is
     among the options in the TRAVELTIME command line, VERBOSE is
     turned off (if it was on) and nothing is displayed on the screen.

   :M | KM:  If M is among the options in the command line, SAC
     interprets EVDP as being in meters.  If KM is displayed, EVDP is
     interpreted as being in kilometers.

   :ONLINE: Retrieve traveltimes from the IRIS Traveltime Web Service.
     This uses TauP internally and allows for a variety of alternative
     phases not possible with the internal models distributed with sac.
     Online phases include ttp, tts, ttp+, tts+, ttbasic, ttall, and
     any phase understandable to TauP; P, S, PcP, SS, pP, PmP, SKJKS.
     See the TauP documentation, reference below.

   :DEFAULT VALUES: MODEL iasp91 KM PHASE P S Pn Pg Sn Sg

DESCRIPTION
-----------
   All waveforms in memory must have event and station locations
   defined as well as the origin time.

   This command calculates traveltimes using the iaspei-tau procedures
   developed for models iasp91 and ak135. For more information on this
   package, go to the  iaspei-tau link at URL
   <https://seiscode.iris.washington.edu/projects/iaspei-tau/>. The
   phase picks  are stored in the SAC header for all files in memory in
   header variables Tn, where n is in the range 0 to 9. The times are
   calculated relative to the origin time (O) but the Tn times are
   relative to the first-point time (B).

   The resulting traveltime curves can be plotted on top of a record
   section plot using the PLOTRECORDSECTION_ command.  If n and the
   number of phases are such that the phase list runs above T9, those
   extra phases are not plotted or recorded in the header, but curves
   will be displayed with the PLOTRECORDSECTION command if the TTIME
   option is on.

   For historical reason, SAC waveforms produced by RDSEED had EVDP in
   meters. Beginning with v101.5, the default for EVDP was changed to
   kilometers, but as old waveforms may have EVDP in meters, the
   command-line option M means the input EVDP for all files in memory
   have EVDP inmeters. In RDSEED v5.2 or later, EVDEP is in kilometers.
   For all runs of TRAVELTIME, the depth is printed to the screen.

   Prior to 2005, SAC was developed and maintained by LLNL.  TRAVELTIME
   did not work in IRIS SAC until v105 because of endian problems with
   the model files and priorities of the SAC developers).  Unlike the
   pre-2005 SA2000, TRAVELTIME works in both SSS and without going into
   SSS.

   From sac-help entries in 2019, it became apparent that some phase
   choices such as SKS or PKP give error messages rather than arrival
   times.  Upon closer examination, we find that all explicit phases
   the produce arrival times for a depth and distance in iaspei-tau
   program TTIMES produce arrival times in SAC TRAVELTIME.  The problem
   with the LLNL SAC2000 TRAVELTIME program is that the keywords used
   in TTIMES, such as "P", P+", and "all", are in the code for
   TRAVELTIME but do not work.  Now that we understand the problem, it
   will be fixed in a future version of SAC.  In the meantime, TTIMES
   should help one find the phase names that can then be entered into
   TRAVELTIME.  The examples below make ue of that approach.

EXAMPLES
--------

  A regional event using the default phases::

     SAC> fg seismo
     SAC> SSS
     Signal Stacking Subprocess.
     SAC/SSS>  traveltime
     traveltime: depth: 15.000000
     traveltime: error finding phase P
     traveltime: error finding phase S
     traveltime: setting phase Pn    at 10.464321 s [ t = 51.894321 s ]
     traveltime: setting phase Pg    at 22.904724 s [ t = 64.334724 s ]
     traveltime: setting phase Sn    at 50.047722 s [ t = 91.477722 s ]
     traveltime: setting phase Sg    at 66.414337 s [ t = 107.844337 s ]
     SAC> lh picks
     NPTS = 1000                   B = 9.459999e+00
     E = 1.945000e+01              OMARKER = -41.43
     AMARKER = 10.464              KZDATE = MAR 29 (088), 1981
     KZTIME = 10:38:14.000

  For regional events the first arrivals are Pn or Pg, so by the convention used
  by LLNL, here there is no direct "P" arival.  This is an example of a choice
  made at LLNL  that can lead to confusion: For TTIME, entering P and S include
  these phases and many others.

  For waveform seis, the focal depth is 15 km, and the epicdentral
  distance is 373 km. Program TTIMES finds 31 arrivals for an event
  with this EVDEP and DIST.  Because the final time E is about 60
  seconds after the origin time, phase Pn is the only default phase
  that arrives before E.  However, TTIMES tells us there are four more
  before E::

     SAC> fg seismo
     SAC> SSS
     Signal Stacking Subprocess.
     SAC/SSS>  traveltime picks 0 phase Pn pPn sPn Pb PnPn
     traveltime: depth: 15.000000
     SAC> lh picks
     NPTS = 1000                   B = 9.459999e+00
     E = 1.945000e+01              OMARKER = -41.43
     AMARKER = 10.464              T0MARKER = 10.464     (Pn)
     T1MARKER = 14.064      (pPn)  T2MARKER = 16.324     (sPn)
     T3MARKER = 17.776      (Pb)   T4MARKER = 17.987     (PnPn)
     KZDATE = MAR 29 (088), 1981   KZTIME = 10:38:14.000
     SAC> write seismo-picks.z

  We see that the already defined A is at Pn.  The  output
  seismo-picks.z will have T0 through T4 in the header, and a call to PLOT1_
  will show labeled vertical lines at the calculated times.  When doing multiple
  runs of TRAVLETIME with the same data set, recall that the SAC convention is
  not to reset command-line arguments to the default between runs, so it is
  perhaps wise to quit and restart.

  The printing of traveltime depth can be suppressed by entering QUIET on the
  command line.

  The header for the waveform in the following example was produced by RDSEED
  (V5.0) and EVDP is in meters::

     SAC> r 2008.052.14.16.03.0000.XC.OR075.00.LHZ.M.SAC
     SAC> lh evdp
     evdp = 6.700000e+03
     SAC> SSS
     Signal Stacking Subprocess.
     SAC/SSS>  traveltime M picks 0
     traveltime: depth: 6.700000 km
     SAC> lh t0marker t1marker t2marker t3marker
     t0marker = 61.48       (Pn)        t1marker = 76.413      (Pg)
     t2marker = 109.66      (Sn)        t3marker = 132.11      (Sg)
     SAC> ch evdp (0.001 * &1,evdp&)
     SAC> setbb station &1,KSTNM&
     SAC> write %station%.z

  The saved file, OR075.z, will have evdp in kilometers and annotated picks at
  the times for Pn, Pg, Sn, and Sg.  (One could have done the chnhdr command
  before calling TRAVELTME and then not entered M on the command line.)

  The final example shows phase arrivals for a deep (611 km) event recorded at
  5.824949e+03 km.  TTIMES found 16 arrivals between B and E.  The seven most
  prominent are included in this call to TRAVELTIME and are shown in the plot
  below::

    SAC> r KEV.dLHR
    SAC> SSS
    Signal Stacking Subprocess.
    SAC/SSS>  traveltime picks 0 phase P pP PP sP ScP PcS S SPn PKiKP
    traveltime: depth: 611.00 km
    SAC> lh picks
    NPTS = 1001                        B = 3.998600e+02
    E = 1.399860e+03                   OMARKER = 0
    T0MARKER = 497.45      (P)         T1MARKER = 609.92      (pP)
    T2MARKER = 676.41      (sP)        T3MARKER = 899.84      (S)
    T4MARKER = 1022.9      (ScS)       T5MARKER = 953.59      (PKiKP)
    T6MARKER = 1131.4      (SS)        KZDATE = MAY 12 (132), 1990
    KZTIME = 04:50:08.900

  A call traveltime phase PKP would have produced an error message, but PKiKP
  works.  This seismogram is used in the iaspei-tau package documentation.

